# What must NOT reach the image. Every line here is a thing that either leaks or
# bloats: `COPY . .` copies the build context as it is on the machine that runs
# the build, and an image, once it leaves that machine, cannot be called back.

# --- Version control, editors, caches ---------------------------------------
.git
.github
.venv
.idea
.vscode
.DS_Store
__pycache__
*.pyc
.pytest_cache
.ruff_cache
catboost_info
dist
node_modules

# --- Secrets ----------------------------------------------------------------
# A GLOB, not the two names we happen to use today. `.env` and `.env.docker` were
# listed and `.env.local` / `.env.staging` / `.env.prod.bak` were not - those went
# into the image, and `docker history` / `docker cp` hands the SMTP password and the
# bot token to anyone who has it. Settings are passed at RUN time (compose
# `env_file`), so no .env belongs in a layer, examples included.
.env*

# --- Student data -----------------------------------------------------------
# data/ is IGNORED WHOLESALE and the one file the container genuinely needs is
# re-included below. The deny-list version of this rule is what the audit found:
# .gitignore commits `data/*.csv` on purpose (so `git clone` runs immediately), so
# on the day a real customer export is dropped into data/, every image built from
# that checkout carries those student records - and nobody would have edited this
# file first. An allow-list makes the safe answer the default for a new file.
# `data/*` + `!` rather than `data/`: the documented Docker idiom for "exclude the
# contents, keep one file", and the one that does not depend on how the builder
# treats an excluded directory - this re-include is load-bearing for the demo.
data/*
# The daily serving sample: `scripts/load_daily_students.py` (the init container)
# reads it, and DATA_SOURCE=csv serves from it. Everything else in data/, including
# the alert log and any real export, stays out.
!data/daily_data.csv

# The training export and the engineered frame stay out: nothing in the serving
# image reads them (training runs on a workstation and ships the model in
# saved_models/), and they are the two largest, most personal files in the repo.
# `python running_train_pipeline.py` therefore does not work inside the container -
# deliberately. See README "Container hijyeni".

# Regenerated per run; the CSV-mode alert log is written into a directory the
# container owns at runtime and the scheduler's state lives on a volume.
metrics
state

# --- Not part of the product ------------------------------------------------
# Experiments (they import config and would be shipped as if they were supported),
# notebooks and their checkpoints, and any model weights format that is not the
# CatBoost file the API loads.
RnD
notebooks
*.ipynb
.ipynb_checkpoints
*.pt
*.pth
tests
docs
